{"id":"coding-functions","title":"Coding: functions","subject":"Coding","label":"Coding","exams":[],"language":"en","seconds":158,"duration":158.36,"audio":"voice.mp3","words":443,"sections":[{"id":"hook","title":"Everyday puzzle","start":0,"end":15.025},{"id":"explain","title":"Core idea","start":15.025,"end":39.035},{"id":"explain2","title":"Return and print","start":39.035,"end":61.235},{"id":"example","title":"Worked example","start":61.235,"end":84.555},{"id":"check","title":"Quick check","start":84.555,"end":93.685},{"id":"answer","title":"Answer","start":93.685,"end":98.625},{"id":"mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","start":98.625,"end":130.005},{"id":"recap","title":"Recap","start":130.005,"end":158.36}],"lines":[{"s":0,"a":0.5,"b":5.02,"text":"Have you ever solved the same small problem twice and wished you could reuse the answer?","w":[0.52,0.66,0.84,1.08,1.42,1.58,1.92,2.26,2.66,3.36,3.5,3.76,3.9,4.08,4.46,4.64]},{"s":0,"a":5.47,"b":8.99,"text":"Imagine writing out the same recipe every single time you cook.","w":[5.56,6,6.32,6.48,6.64,6.94,7.74,8.06,8.38,8.64,8.78]},{"s":0,"a":9.29,"b":9.93,"text":"I'm Kora.","w":[9.36,9.56]},{"s":0,"a":10.23,"b":14.6,"text":"Today you'll learn how coders name a piece of work once, then use it again and again.","w":[10.28,10.62,10.88,11.12,11.34,11.74,11.96,12.02,12.24,12.34,12.66,13.24,13.54,13.7,13.8,14.1,14.22]},{"s":1,"a":15.45,"b":18.46,"text":"A function is a named block of code that does one job.","w":[15.5,15.6,16.08,16.22,16.3,16.66,17,17.12,17.42,17.58,17.9,18.06]},{"s":1,"a":18.76,"b":21.78,"text":"You define it once, then call it whenever you need it.","w":[18.78,18.94,19.38,19.6,20.26,20.52,20.78,20.88,21.24,21.38,21.58]},{"s":1,"a":22.08,"b":26.48,"text":"In Python, you start with the word def, then a name, then brackets.","w":[22.16,22.3,22.82,23.02,23.3,23.44,23.56,23.84,24.6,24.84,24.94,25.6,25.86]},{"s":1,"a":26.78,"b":28.38,"text":"The brackets can hold parameters.","w":[26.82,26.94,27.36,27.52,27.74]},{"s":1,"a":28.68,"b":30.91,"text":"Parameters are the names in the definition.","w":[28.72,29.42,29.54,29.7,30.14,30.24,30.34]},{"s":1,"a":31.21,"b":34.41,"text":"Arguments are the actual values you pass in when calling.","w":[31.28,31.82,31.94,32.2,32.62,33.06,33.22,33.58,33.76,33.98]},{"s":1,"a":34.71,"b":38.61,"text":"So the definition describes the work, and the call supplies the values.","w":[34.8,34.96,35.08,35.6,36.08,36.2,36.94,37.06,37.2,37.54,37.94,38.06]},{"s":2,"a":39.46,"b":44.09,"text":"Return sends a result back to the place that called it, and the function stops there.","w":[39.52,40.28,40.56,40.64,41.08,41.46,41.58,41.68,41.98,42.14,42.44,42.86,42.98,43.1,43.5,43.84]},{"s":2,"a":44.39,"b":46.45,"text":"Print only shows something on the screen.","w":[44.44,44.82,45.1,45.44,45.82,45.92,46.04]},{"s":2,"a":46.75,"b":50.29,"text":"Return gives the value back, so your program can keep using it.","w":[46.82,47.22,47.46,47.58,47.92,48.66,48.8,48.96,49.4,49.56,49.84,50.1]},{"s":2,"a":50.59,"b":53.49,"text":"A function with no return gives back None in Python.","w":[50.66,50.78,51.18,51.42,51.62,52.12,52.4,52.7,52.92,53.02]},{"s":2,"a":53.79,"b":58.39,"text":"Functions stop repetition, make code easier to test, and easier to read.","w":[53.86,54.54,54.84,55.76,55.94,56.24,56.58,56.7,57.48,57.64,57.96,58.08]},{"s":2,"a":58.69,"b":60.81,"text":"That is why we say don't repeat yourself.","w":[58.72,58.94,59.08,59.36,59.54,59.82,60.08,60.4]},{"s":3,"a":61.66,"b":62.73,"text":"Here is a tiny example.","w":[61.68,61.86,61.96,62.02,62.34]},{"s":3,"a":63.03,"b":65.36,"text":"Define area with parameters width and height.","w":[63.03,63.56,63.86,64.04,64.68,64.92,65.04]},{"s":3,"a":65.66,"b":68.6,"text":"Inside the function, return width times height.","w":[65.74,66.18,66.28,67.04,67.52,67.88,68.22]},{"s":3,"a":68.9,"b":70.99,"text":"Now call area with four and five.","w":[68.96,69.22,69.58,69.92,70.14,70.44,70.6]},{"s":3,"a":71.29,"b":72.25,"text":"That gives twenty.","w":[71.32,71.52,71.82]},{"s":3,"a":72.55,"b":76.48,"text":"Store twenty in a variable, say size, and use it again later.","w":[72.68,73.02,73.4,73.5,73.58,74.4,74.7,75.44,75.66,75.8,75.9,76.16]},{"s":3,"a":76.78,"b":79.31,"text":"You wrote the work once, and you can reuse it.","w":[76.82,77,77.22,77.34,77.72,78.32,78.46,78.6,78.76,79.2]},{"s":3,"a":79.61,"b":84.13,"text":"If you need the area of another rectangle, call area again with new values.","w":[79.68,79.78,79.94,80.14,80.32,80.66,80.76,81.12,82.08,82.44,82.8,83.14,83.38,83.54]},{"s":4,"a":84.98,"b":85.54,"text":"Quick check.","w":[85,85.24]},{"s":4,"a":85.84,"b":88.87,"text":"A function double takes n and returns n times two.","w":[85.94,86.04,86.46,86.8,87.26,87.46,87.6,88.16,88.3,88.62]},{"s":4,"a":89.17,"b":90.68,"text":"What does double of seven give?","w":[89.24,89.42,89.66,90.02,90.14,90.44]},{"s":4,"a":91.13,"b":93.26,"text":"Pause here and try it. I'll wait.","w":[91.14,91.48,91.8,91.94,92.2,92.74,92.92]},{"s":5,"a":94.11,"b":94.7,"text":"Fourteen.","w":[94.14]},{"s":5,"a":95,"b":98.2,"text":"Double of seven is seven times two, which is fourteen.","w":[95,95.32,95.46,96,96.16,96.48,96.82,97.32,97.52,97.66]},{"s":6,"a":99.05,"b":100.51,"text":"Three common mistakes.","w":[99.12,99.48,99.82]},{"s":6,"a":100.81,"b":103.61,"text":"First, defining a function but never calling it.","w":[100.9,101.56,102.08,102.18,102.74,102.9,103.16,103.5]},{"s":6,"a":103.91,"b":107.67,"text":"A definition alone does nothing, so call it where you need it.","w":[103.98,104.08,104.72,105.16,105.46,106.32,106.54,106.82,106.92,107.1,107.26,107.48]},{"s":6,"a":107.97,"b":110.27,"text":"Second, confusing print with return.","w":[108.08,108.7,109.34,109.64,109.82]},{"s":6,"a":110.57,"b":113.94,"text":"Print shows a value, but return gives it back to your program.","w":[110.58,110.88,111.18,111.24,111.9,112.06,112.56,112.82,112.94,113.18,113.28,113.46]},{"s":6,"a":114.24,"b":119.23,"text":"Third, forgetting to pass an argument, or writing one giant function that does everything.","w":[114.32,115.06,115.48,115.6,115.9,116.02,116.8,116.92,117.36,117.56,117.96,118.36,118.52,118.8]},{"s":6,"a":119.53,"b":123.42,"text":"Pass what the function needs, and keep each function focused on one job.","w":[119.54,119.86,120.02,120.14,120.54,121.26,121.38,121.68,121.9,122.4,122.8,122.98,123.12]},{"s":6,"a":123.72,"b":127.36,"text":"If a function needs two values, pass both in the right order.","w":[123.76,123.9,124,124.46,124.76,124.98,125.76,126.18,126.54,126.64,126.76,127.06]},{"s":6,"a":127.66,"b":129.58,"text":"Test small functions one at a time.","w":[127.68,128.02,128.34,128.92,129.06,129.16,129.24]},{"s":7,"a":130.43,"b":130.99,"text":"Recap.","w":[130.5]},{"s":7,"a":131.29,"b":136.54,"text":"A function names one job so you can reuse it. Define once, call many times.","w":[131.38,131.48,131.88,132.32,132.5,133.2,133.38,133.52,133.68,134.06,134.66,135.2,135.54,135.82,136.06]},{"s":7,"a":136.84,"b":141.59,"text":"Parameters are the names in the definition, and arguments are the values you pass.","w":[136.88,137.52,137.66,137.82,138.12,138.2,138.32,139.32,139.56,140.08,140.2,140.34,140.86,141.04]},{"s":7,"a":141.89,"b":149.67,"text":"Return sends a result back, while print only displays it. Keep functions small and focused, so testing and reading stay easier.","w":[141.94,142.42,142.7,142.78,143.16,143.78,144.02,144.38,144.6,145.06,145.62,145.9,146.36,146.72,146.88,147.86,148.06,148.46,148.58,148.94,149.26]},{"s":7,"a":149.97,"b":153.89,"text":"Top tip: if you write similar code twice, make it a function.","w":[149.98,150.32,150.96,151.04,151.2,151.5,151.88,152.18,153.12,153.32,153.4,153.48]},{"s":7,"a":154.19,"b":157.76,"text":"Knowzone is always here whenever you want to practise again with us.","w":[154.24,154.78,155.06,155.42,155.74,156.08,156.22,156.42,156.56,157.02,157.26,157.48]}],"scenes":[{"at":0.25,"heading":"Everyday puzzle","items":[{"at":0.4,"kind":"text","text":"Solved the same small problem twice?"},{"at":5.37,"kind":"text","text":"Writing the same recipe every time?"},{"at":9.19,"kind":"text","text":"Today: functions"},{"at":10.13,"kind":"text","text":"Name work once; use it again and again."}]},{"at":15.2,"heading":"Core idea","items":[{"at":15.35,"kind":"term","text":"Function","detail":"A named block of code that does one job."},{"at":18.66,"kind":"text","text":"Define once, call whenever needed."},{"at":21.98,"kind":"formula","text":"def name():"},{"at":26.68,"kind":"text","text":"Brackets can hold parameters."}]},{"at":28.43,"heading":"Parameters vs arguments","items":[{"at":28.58,"kind":"term","text":"Parameters","detail":"Names in the definition."},{"at":31.11,"kind":"term","text":"Arguments","detail":"Actual values passed when calling."},{"at":34.61,"kind":"text","text":"Definition describes work; call supplies values."}]},{"at":39.21,"heading":"Return vs print","items":[{"at":39.36,"kind":"term","text":"Return","detail":"Sends a result back; function stops there."},{"at":44.29,"kind":"term","text":"Print","detail":"Only shows something on the screen."},{"at":50.49,"kind":"text","text":"No return gives None in Python."},{"at":53.69,"kind":"text","text":"Stop repetition; easier to test and read."},{"at":58.59,"kind":"text","text":"Don't repeat yourself."}]},{"at":61.41,"heading":"Worked example: area","items":[{"at":62.93,"kind":"formula","text":"def area(width, height):"},{"at":65.56,"kind":"formula","text":"    return width * height"},{"at":68.8,"kind":"formula","text":"area(4, 5)"},{"at":71.19,"kind":"formula","text":"area(4, 5) gives 20"},{"at":72.45,"kind":"formula","text":"size = area(4, 5)"}]},{"at":76.53,"heading":"Reuse","items":[{"at":76.68,"kind":"text","text":"Wrote the work once; reuse it."},{"at":79.51,"kind":"text","text":"Call area again with new values."}]},{"at":84.56,"heading":"Quick check","items":[{"at":84.56,"kind":"question","text":"A function double takes n and returns n × 2. What does double(7) give?"}]},{"at":93.86,"heading":"Answer","items":[{"at":94.01,"kind":"text","text":"14"},{"at":94.9,"kind":"formula","text":"7 × 2 = 14"}]},{"at":98.8,"heading":"Common mistakes","items":[{"at":103.81,"kind":"wrong","text":"Defined but never called"},{"at":110.47,"kind":"wrong","text":"Print confused with return"},{"at":119.43,"kind":"wrong","text":"Forgetting an argument; one giant function"},{"at":123.62,"kind":"wrong","text":"Pass two values in wrong order"},{"at":127.56,"kind":"text","text":"Test small functions one at a time."}]},{"at":130.18,"heading":"Recap","items":[{"at":131.19,"kind":"text","text":"Function names one job; define once, call many times."},{"at":136.74,"kind":"term","text":"Parameters vs arguments","detail":"Names in the definition vs values passed when calling."},{"at":141.79,"kind":"text","text":"Return sends back; print only displays."},{"at":149.87,"kind":"tip","text":"If you write similar code twice, make it a function."},{"at":154.09,"kind":"text","text":"Knowzone: practise again whenever you want."}]}],"check":{"at":93.51,"seconds":10,"question":"A function double takes n and returns n × 2. What does double(7) give?","answer":"14 (7 × 2 = 14)"},"fps":30,"mouth":"","level":"","credits":[],"voice":"qwen3-tts-12hz-1.7b (cloned voice)"}